# PHASE 3 — Define the Speed Test Lifecycle

Create one clear test state system.

```text id="mymqxh"
IDLE
  ↓
CONNECTING
  ↓
SELECTING_SERVER
  ↓
TESTING_PING
  ↓
TESTING_DOWNLOAD
  ↓
TESTING_UPLOAD
  ↓
COMPLETED
```

Error states:

```text id="4aun1t"
ERROR
CANCELLED
```

## State Rules

The UI must always reflect the real measurement state.

Examples:

### Before test

```text id="b7y0p0"
Ready to test your internet speed

[ Start Test ]
```

### Download

```text id="ft8ntd"
Testing Download Speed
Time remaining: 5s
Current speed: 245 Mbps
```

### Upload

```text id="x9f6w1"
Testing Upload Speed
Time remaining: 4s
Current speed: 48 Mbps
```

### Completed

```text id="9r6pup"
Speed Test Complete
```

---

# PHASE 4 — Configure Test Duration

## Objective

Configure the speed-test execution duration correctly.

### Required Configuration

```text id="slb8x9"
Ping Test
→ Use backend/default measurement configuration

Download Test
→ Approximately 7 seconds

Upload Test
→ Approximately 7 seconds
```

## Important Implementation Rule

The **7-second duration must be applied to the actual measurement process**, not only to a frontend timer.

Correct flow:

```text id="zdb1nr"
Backend Measurement Duration
        ↓
Actual Network Test
        ↓
Live Measurement Data
        ↓
Frontend UI
        ↓
Countdown / Progress Display
```

Incorrect implementation:

```text id="ck0iyp"
Fake 7-second UI Timer
        ↓
Measurement continues differently
```

### Requirements

* Verify how LibreSpeed controls download duration.
* Verify how LibreSpeed controls upload duration.
* Configure the actual backend/client test duration.
* Keep both download and upload tests close to 7 seconds.
* Do not terminate the test based only on frontend animation.
* The displayed countdown must synchronize with the actual test duration.

